{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PGeGVaHd4i1sxFYtPQNk4a","type":"tool_result","content":"=== CH2: immutable ===\n95:    - \"immutability\"\n334:### Immutability, and why it's a rule rather than a preference\n336:Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable: you should not change the code of a container that is already running. If you have a containerized application and want to make changes, the correct process is to build a new image that includes the change, then recreate the container to start from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n342:The payoff is concrete. Image immutability is exactly what makes quick and efficient rollbacks possible [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]. You can go back to the previous version because the previous version still exists, unchanged, as a distinct artifact. Edit containers in place and there is nothing to go back to.\n365:  ├─────────┤  ├─────────┤             │   immutable              │\n423:Tags let you identify different versions of the same series of images. Digests are a unique identifier for a specific version of an image, **a hash of the image's content**, and are immutable; **tags can be moved to point to different images** [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n431:> **A tag identifies a series and can be moved to point at a different image. A digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable. Two pulls of `myapp:v2` a week apart are not guaranteed to be the same bytes. Two pulls of an image by digest are.**\n484:B) The bytes are guaranteed identical, because tags are immutable by specification\n504:**2 — B.** Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable; the correct process to make a change is to build a new image that includes it, then recreate the container from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n507:- **D is wrong.** The Pod spec references an image; it is not a place to record file edits, and re-applying an edit on every restart is exactly the repair-in-place habit immutability replaces.\n509:**3 — C.** Tags can be moved to point to different images [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. An identical reference is not a guarantee of identical content; only a digest gives you that, because a digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n511:- **B is wrong** and inverts the specification: digests are immutable; tags are movable.\n523:✓ What an image contains, what it cannot contain, and why immutability is the process rather than a preference\n781:> 🪝 **Snag:** Once a Pod is created, `imagePullPolicy` is not updated if the image's tag or digest changes later [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. The policy was resolved when the Pod was created, and moving the tag afterwards does not retroactively change how that existing Pod behaves. If you need new behavior, you need a new Pod, which is §2's immutability principle showing up in an unexpected place.\n922:1. **Tag versus digest.** A tag identifies a series and can be moved. A digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. Every claim anyone makes about reproducible deployment rests on this distinction.\n937:| \"You patch a running container\" | §2 immutability — the correct process is build a new image, then recreate [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23]; Bearings #1 Q2 |\n1027:A) A tag is immutable; a digest can be re-pointed\n1029:C) Both are immutable once pushed to a registry\n1168:**5 — B.** Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable; if you want to make changes, the correct process is to build a new image that includes the change, then recreate the container to start from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n1199:**11 — B.** Digests are a unique identifier for a specific version of an image, a hash of the image's content, and are immutable; tags can be moved to point to different images [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n1292:| Immutability | Don't change a running container. Build a new image, recreate the container |\n1296:| Tag vs digest | **A tag is a movable label. A digest is a content hash and is immutable** |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f222f12b-34e7-4afa-8c6b-e6a91ea0bddb","uuid":"e26ddf3a-09a3-498e-a757-b0c50a807372","timestamp":"2026-08-24T09:41:15.785Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== CH2: immutable ===\n95:    - \"immutability\"\n334:### Immutability, and why it's a rule rather than a preference\n336:Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable: you should not change the code of a container that is already running. If you have a containerized application and want to make changes, the correct process is to build a new image that includes the change, then recreate the container to start from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n342:The payoff is concrete. Image immutability is exactly what makes quick and efficient rollbacks possible [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]. You can go back to the previous version because the previous version still exists, unchanged, as a distinct artifact. Edit containers in place and there is nothing to go back to.\n365:  ├─────────┤  ├─────────┤             │   immutable              │\n423:Tags let you identify different versions of the same series of images. Digests are a unique identifier for a specific version of an image, **a hash of the image's content**, and are immutable; **tags can be moved to point to different images** [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n431:> **A tag identifies a series and can be moved to point at a different image. A digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable. Two pulls of `myapp:v2` a week apart are not guaranteed to be the same bytes. Two pulls of an image by digest are.**\n484:B) The bytes are guaranteed identical, because tags are immutable by specification\n504:**2 — B.** Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable; the correct process to make a change is to build a new image that includes it, then recreate the container from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n507:- **D is wrong.** The Pod spec references an image; it is not a place to record file edits, and re-applying an edit on every restart is exactly the repair-in-place habit immutability replaces.\n509:**3 — C.** Tags can be moved to point to different images [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. An identical reference is not a guarantee of identical content; only a digest gives you that, because a digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n511:- **B is wrong** and inverts the specification: digests are immutable; tags are movable.\n523:✓ What an image contains, what it cannot contain, and why immutability is the process rather than a preference\n781:> 🪝 **Snag:** Once a Pod is created, `imagePullPolicy` is not updated if the image's tag or digest changes later [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. The policy was resolved when the Pod was created, and moving the tag afterwards does not retroactively change how that existing Pod behaves. If you need new behavior, you need a new Pod, which is §2's immutability principle showing up in an unexpected place.\n922:1. **Tag versus digest.** A tag identifies a series and can be moved. A digest is a hash of the image's content and is immutable [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23]. Every claim anyone makes about reproducible deployment rests on this distinction.\n937:| \"You patch a running container\" | §2 immutability — the correct process is build a new image, then recreate [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23]; Bearings #1 Q2 |\n1027:A) A tag is immutable; a digest can be re-pointed\n1029:C) Both are immutable once pushed to a registry\n1168:**5 — B.** Containers are intended to be stateless and immutable; if you want to make changes, the correct process is to build a new image that includes the change, then recreate the container to start from the updated image [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n1199:**11 — B.** Digests are a unique identifier for a specific version of an image, a hash of the image's content, and are immutable; tags can be moved to point to different images [source: k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23].\n1292:| Immutability | Don't change a running container. Build a new image, recreate the container |\n1296:| Tag vs digest | **A tag is a movable label. A digest is a content hash and is immutable** |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
