{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WiFGdKxWfGToxYfu4A4ZTh","type":"tool_result","content":"454:[Omitted long matching line]\n460:A **ConfigMap** is an API object used to store **non-confidential** data in key-value pairs. Pods can consume ConfigMaps as environment variables, command-line arguments, or as configuration files in a volume. A ConfigMap allows you to decouple environment-specific configuration from your container images, so that your applications are easily portable [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n464:**The size ceiling.** A ConfigMap is not designed to hold large chunks of data; the data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed **1 MiB**. For settings larger than that, the guidance is to mount a volume, or use a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n466:**The namespace requirement.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. That is §3's scope-for-names rule showing up one section later in the place you actually trip over it: a Pod naming a ConfigMap is naming it inside its own scope.\n468:[Omitted long matching line]\n470:> 🪝 **Snag:** You edited the ConfigMap. The running application did not notice. This is the single most common day-one surprise with ConfigMaps, and it is not a bug: three of the four consumption paths are applied by the kubelet *at container launch*. Only the fourth, where your code reads the Kubernetes API itself, subscribes to changes [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. If you want the first three to pick up a change, something has to cause the containers to be launched again.\n472:[Omitted long matching line]\n474:And one caution stated in the documentation with unusual directness, which sets up the rest of this section: **ConfigMap does not provide secrecy or encryption.** If the data you want to store are confidential, use a Secret rather than a ConfigMap, or use additional third-party tools to keep your data private [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n498:[Omitted long matching line]\n535:<!-- FIGURE: ch04-fig05-configmap-secret-contrast -->\n568:> **\"Update the ConfigMap and the running container picks it up.\"** Only if the application reads the Kubernetes API directly. The other three consumption paths are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n570:> **\"Immutable can be turned back off.\"** It cannot. Marking a ConfigMap immutable is irreversible; delete and recreate is the only route back [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n572:> **\"A ConfigMap is a fine place for that file.\"** Up to 1 MiB of data. Past that, use a volume, a database, or a file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n574:> **\"Pods in `team-a` can reference the ConfigMap in `team-b`.\"** They cannot. The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. That is §3's scope-for-names rule showing up where you actually trip over it.\n613:**3. Answer: D.** Only the API-reading path lets the application subscribe to updates whenever the ConfigMap changes [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n874:| Assuming a ConfigMap change reaches a running container | Only via the API-reading path. The other three are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n875:| \"An immutable ConfigMap can be un-marked\" | It cannot be reverted. Delete and recreate [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n876:| Forgetting the ConfigMap size ceiling | 1 MiB [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n877:| Referencing a ConfigMap across namespaces | The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n907:C) It writes the new Pod objects into etcd directly, since it is a control-plane component\n1048:- **C** picks a real control-plane component with the wrong job; the scheduler watches for Pods with no assigned node and selects nodes for them [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. It receives nothing from `kubectl`.\n1057:- **B** invents a controller-to-kubelet channel. Chapter 3's hub-and-spoke pattern rules it out: all API usage terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components is designed to expose remote services [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24].\n1058:- **C** is the one that survives longest for people who half-remember the architecture. Being a control-plane component does not grant direct access to the store. Ideally only the API server has access to etcd, since access to etcd is equivalent to root permission in the cluster [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24].\n1096:- **B** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n1099:**Q12. Answer: A.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n1103:**Q13. [retrieval: ch2] Answer: B.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1108:**Q14. Answer: A.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1113:**Q15. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f222f12b-34e7-4afa-8c6b-e6a91ea0bddb","uuid":"0187dc76-af78-4fae-9dda-f8da4b5cf9d7","timestamp":"2026-08-24T09:42:45.836Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"454:[Omitted long matching line]\n460:A **ConfigMap** is an API object used to store **non-confidential** data in key-value pairs. Pods can consume ConfigMaps as environment variables, command-line arguments, or as configuration files in a volume. A ConfigMap allows you to decouple environment-specific configuration from your container images, so that your applications are easily portable [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n464:**The size ceiling.** A ConfigMap is not designed to hold large chunks of data; the data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed **1 MiB**. For settings larger than that, the guidance is to mount a volume, or use a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n466:**The namespace requirement.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. That is §3's scope-for-names rule showing up one section later in the place you actually trip over it: a Pod naming a ConfigMap is naming it inside its own scope.\n468:[Omitted long matching line]\n470:> 🪝 **Snag:** You edited the ConfigMap. The running application did not notice. This is the single most common day-one surprise with ConfigMaps, and it is not a bug: three of the four consumption paths are applied by the kubelet *at container launch*. Only the fourth, where your code reads the Kubernetes API itself, subscribes to changes [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. If you want the first three to pick up a change, something has to cause the containers to be launched again.\n472:[Omitted long matching line]\n474:And one caution stated in the documentation with unusual directness, which sets up the rest of this section: **ConfigMap does not provide secrecy or encryption.** If the data you want to store are confidential, use a Secret rather than a ConfigMap, or use additional third-party tools to keep your data private [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n498:[Omitted long matching line]\n535:<!-- FIGURE: ch04-fig05-configmap-secret-contrast -->\n568:> **\"Update the ConfigMap and the running container picks it up.\"** Only if the application reads the Kubernetes API directly. The other three consumption paths are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n570:> **\"Immutable can be turned back off.\"** It cannot. Marking a ConfigMap immutable is irreversible; delete and recreate is the only route back [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n572:> **\"A ConfigMap is a fine place for that file.\"** Up to 1 MiB of data. Past that, use a volume, a database, or a file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n574:> **\"Pods in `team-a` can reference the ConfigMap in `team-b`.\"** They cannot. The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. That is §3's scope-for-names rule showing up where you actually trip over it.\n613:**3. Answer: D.** Only the API-reading path lets the application subscribe to updates whenever the ConfigMap changes [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n874:| Assuming a ConfigMap change reaches a running container | Only via the API-reading path. The other three are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n875:| \"An immutable ConfigMap can be un-marked\" | It cannot be reverted. Delete and recreate [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n876:| Forgetting the ConfigMap size ceiling | 1 MiB [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n877:| Referencing a ConfigMap across namespaces | The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n907:C) It writes the new Pod objects into etcd directly, since it is a control-plane component\n1048:- **C** picks a real control-plane component with the wrong job; the scheduler watches for Pods with no assigned node and selects nodes for them [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. It receives nothing from `kubectl`.\n1057:- **B** invents a controller-to-kubelet channel. Chapter 3's hub-and-spoke pattern rules it out: all API usage terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components is designed to expose remote services [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24].\n1058:- **C** is the one that survives longest for people who half-remember the architecture. Being a control-plane component does not grant direct access to the store. Ideally only the API server has access to etcd, since access to etcd is equivalent to root permission in the cluster [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24].\n1096:- **B** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n1099:**Q12. Answer: A.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n1103:**Q13. [retrieval: ch2] Answer: B.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1108:**Q14. Answer: A.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1113:**Q15. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`.","numLines":28,"totalLines":28}}
